    expectedAnswer: "Compound Components expose a small family of components that share state through a parent-owned contract. They are useful for APIs such as Tabs, Accordion, Select, and Menu where the consumer needs flexible composition while the parent owns the shared behavior.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the React pattern.\n\nStep 1 — Keep shared state inside the compound parent.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface TabsState {\n  active: string;\n}\n\ninterface TabsController {\n  state: TabsState;\n  select(id: string): void;\n}\n\nfunction createTabsController(initial: string): TabsController {\n  const state: TabsState = { active: initial };\n\n  return {\n    state,\n    select(id) {\n      state.active = id;\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nconst tabs = createTabsController(\"profile\");\ntabs.select(\"settings\");\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / React-style context model:\n```ts\ninterface TabsContext {\n  active: string;\n  select(id: string): void;\n}\n\nfunction createTabsContext(initial: string): TabsContext {\n  let active = initial;\n\n  return {\n    get active() {\n      return active;\n    },\n    select(id) {\n      active = id;\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n`Tabs.Root`, `Tabs.List`, `Tabs.Trigger`, and `Tabs.Panel` can share one state contract without forcing every value through props.\n\nComplexity:\nState access is O(1) for the small example; the real concern is component re-render scope.\n\nTrade-off:\nThe API is expressive, but parent/child coupling must be documented and tested.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the state ownership, component API, re-render implications, and why a simpler alternative may be better.",
