    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the React pattern.\n\nStep 1 — Add behavior without changing the original component.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface ViewProps {\n  title: string;\n}\n\ninterface AuthProps {\n  user: string;\n}\n\ntype Component<P> = (props: P) => string;\n\nfunction withAuth<P>(\n  component: Component<P & AuthProps>,\n  user: string | null,\n): Component<P> {\n  return (props) => {\n    if (user === null) return \"Login required\";\n\n    return component({\n      ...props,\n      user,\n    });\n  };\n}\n\nconst Profile: Component<ViewProps & AuthProps> = ({\n  title,\n  user,\n}) => `${title}: ${user}`;\n\nconst ProtectedProfile = withAuth(Profile, \"Rasik\");\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / composition alternative:\n```ts\nfunction withLoading<T>(\n  value: T,\n  loading: boolean,\n  render: (value: T) => string,\n): string {\n  return loading ? \"Loading...\" : render(value);\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n`withAuth(Profile)` can enforce authorization without duplicating the same guard in every screen.\n\nComplexity:\nThe HOC wrapper adds O(1) decision overhead per render; child rendering remains the dominant cost.\n\nTrade-off:\nHOCs can create wrapper nesting and prop collisions. Custom hooks are usually easier to compose for new logic.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the state ownership, component API, re-render implications, and why a simpler alternative may be better.",
