    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the React pattern.\n\nStep 1 — Define a small reusable behavior contract.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface ToggleState {\n  value: boolean;\n  toggle(): void;\n}\n\nfunction createToggle(initial = false): ToggleState {\n  let value = initial;\n\n  return {\n    get value() {\n      return value;\n    },\n    toggle() {\n      value = !value;\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nconst toggle = createToggle();\ntoggle.toggle();\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / hook-style contract:\n```ts\ninterface CounterHook {\n  count: number;\n  increment(): void;\n}\n\nfunction createCounterHook(initial = 0): CounterHook {\n  let count = initial;\n\n  return {\n    get count() {\n      return count;\n    },\n    increment() {\n      count += 1;\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nconst counter = createCounterHook(5);\ncounter.increment();\n```\n\nExample:\nReal custom hooks such as `useDebounce`, `usePrevious`, `useFetch`, or `useLocalStorage` encapsulate reusable behavior while each component chooses its own UI.\n\nComplexity:\nA hook's own logic is usually O(1); derived computations inside the hook determine the real cost.\n\nTrade-off:\nHooks reduce wrapper nesting, but a large hook can become a dumping ground for unrelated responsibilities.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the state ownership, component API, re-render implications, and why a simpler alternative may be better.",
