    expectedAnswer: "Slot Pattern lets a component accept caller-supplied UI pieces for named regions such as header, footer, actions, or icon areas. It creates reusable layout structure without hard-coding every visual detail.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the React pattern.\n\nStep 1 — Model named slots as values or render functions.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface CardSlots {\n  header: string;\n  body: string;\n  footer?: string;\n}\n\nfunction renderCard(slots: CardSlots): string {\n  return [\n    `HEADER: ${slots.header}`,\n    `BODY: ${slots.body}`,\n    slots.footer ? `FOOTER: ${slots.footer}` : \"\",\n  ]\n    .filter(Boolean)\n    .join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\nconst card = renderCard({\n  header: \"Profile\",\n  body: \"User details\",\n  footer: \"Save\",\n});\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / callback slots:\n```ts\ninterface RenderSlots {\n  header(): string;\n  body(): string;\n}\n\nfunction renderLayout(slots: RenderSlots): string {\n  return `${slots.header()}\\n${slots.body()}`;\n}\n\nconst layout = renderLayout({\n  header: () => \"Profile\",\n  body: () => \"Details\",\n});\n```\n\nExample:\nA dialog can expose `header`, `body`, and `actions` slots so every consumer supplies its own content.\n\nComplexity:\nSlot composition is O(number of slots); consumer rendering remains the dominant UI cost.\n\nTrade-off:\nSlots provide strong layout flexibility, but a component with too many named regions can become difficult to learn.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the state ownership, component API, re-render implications, and why a simpler alternative may be better.",
