    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the React pattern.\n\nStep 1 — Keep the shared behavior independent from the semantic element.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ntype ElementType = \"button\" | \"a\" | \"div\";\n\ninterface PolymorphicProps {\n  as: ElementType;\n  label: string;\n}\n\nfunction renderPrimitive(\n  props: PolymorphicProps,\n): string {\n  return `<${props.as}>${props.label}</${props.as}>`;\n}\n\nconst button = renderPrimitive({\n  as: \"button\",\n  label: \"Save\",\n});\n\nconst link = renderPrimitive({\n  as: \"a\",\n  label: \"Open\",\n});\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / variant mapping:\n```ts\ntype Variant = \"primary\" | \"secondary\";\n\nfunction classFor(variant: Variant): string {\n  return variant === \"primary\"\n    ? \"btn-primary\"\n    : \"btn-secondary\";\n}\n\nconst className = classFor(\"primary\");\n```\n\nExample:\nA `Text` primitive can render as `h1`, `p`, or `span` while keeping typography and spacing rules shared.\n\nComplexity:\nSelecting the element/variant is O(1).\n\nTrade-off:\nPolymorphism improves reuse, but production TypeScript types become more advanced when element-specific props must be forwarded safely.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the state ownership, component API, re-render implications, and why a simpler alternative may be better.",
