    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the React pattern.\n\nStep 1 — Centralize interaction props in getters.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface ToggleProps {\n  open: boolean;\n  getButtonProps(): {\n    role: string;\n    \"aria-expanded\": boolean;\n  };\n}\n\nfunction createToggleProps(open: boolean): ToggleProps {\n  return {\n    open,\n    getButtonProps() {\n      return {\n        role: \"button\",\n        \"aria-expanded\": open,\n      };\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nconst toggle = createToggleProps(false);\nconst buttonProps = toggle.getButtonProps();\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / prop merging:\n```ts\nfunction mergeProps(\n  base: Record<string, unknown>,\n  extra: Record<string, unknown>,\n): Record<string, unknown> {\n  return {\n    ...base,\n    ...extra,\n  };\n}\n\nconst props = mergeProps(\n  { role: \"button\" },\n  { \"data-testid\": \"menu-toggle\" },\n);\n```\n\nExample:\nA headless menu can expose `getButtonProps()` and `getMenuProps()` so consumers apply the correct interaction and accessibility attributes.\n\nComplexity:\nProp generation is O(number of merged properties).\n\nTrade-off:\nProps getters create flexible APIs but require consumers to apply the returned contract correctly.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the state ownership, component API, re-render implications, and why a simpler alternative may be better.",
