    expectedAnswer: "Composition Pattern builds complex React UI by combining small components instead of relying on inheritance. It is the default pattern for many modern React designs because behavior and markup can be assembled at the call site.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the React pattern.\n\nStep 1 — Combine focused UI pieces instead of creating one giant component.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface ButtonConfig {\n  label: string;\n}\n\ninterface ToolbarConfig {\n  buttons: readonly ButtonConfig[];\n}\n\nfunction renderToolbar(\n  config: ToolbarConfig,\n): string {\n  return config.buttons\n    .map((button) => `[${button.label}]`)\n    .join(\" \");\n}\n\nconst toolbarText = renderToolbar({\n  buttons: [\n    { label: \"Save\" },\n    { label: \"Cancel\" },\n  ],\n});\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / simple composition:\n```ts\nfunction composeLabels(\n  parts: readonly string[],\n): string {\n  return parts.join(\" | \");\n}\n\nconst result = composeLabels([\n  \"Header\",\n  \"Content\",\n  \"Actions\",\n]);\n```\n\nExample:\nInstead of one `UserDashboard` containing every mode, compose `Dashboard`, `Toolbar`, `UserCard`, `LoadingState`, and `EmptyState`.\n\nComplexity:\nComposition itself is O(number of composed pieces); actual render cost depends on the component tree.\n\nTrade-off:\nComposition reduces inheritance coupling, but too many tiny components can make a tree harder to scan if boundaries are artificial.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the state ownership, component API, re-render implications, and why a simpler alternative may be better.",
